A try block may throw different types of exceptions, such as:

ArithmeticException,
ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException,
IOException

Since we don't know which exception will occur at runtime, we write multiple catch blocks,
and each catch block handles a specific exception differently.

Example :

try {
    // Code that may throw different exceptions
}
catch (ArithmeticException e) {
    System.out.println("Cannot divide by zero.");
}
catch (ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException e) {
    System.out.println("Invalid array index.");
}
catch (IOException e) {
    System.out.println("File operation failed.");
}

If a division by zero occurs, only the ArithmeticException catch block runs.
If an invalid array index occurs, only the ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException catch block runs.
If a file error occurs, only the IOException catch block runs.

When do we use Multiple Catch Blocks?

We use multiple catch blocks when:

A single try block can throw different types of exceptions.
Each exception requires different handling or a different message.

For example:

ArithmeticException → Show "Cannot divide by zero."
FileNotFoundException → Show "File not found."
SQLException → Roll back the database transaction.

Each exception needs a different response, so separate catch blocks are the right choice.